  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m:
  [32m# Staging serves a self-signed certificate. Verification is only disabled when a[39m
  [32m# caller opts in explicitly, so the production scripts sharing this client keep[39m
  [32m# verifying.[39m
  VERIFY_TLS = os.environ.get([31m"REPORTS_VERIFY_TLS"[39m, [31m"1"[39m) != [31m"0"[39m
  Both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls now pass [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY_TLS[39m.

  [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m alongside the base URL it already sets, so staging skips
  verification. [38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m is untouched and still verifies.

  Why not the literal [38;5;153mverify=False[39m: [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is the shared client, and [38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m uses it against
  [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m for the nightly finance export. Hardcoding [38;5;153mFalse[39m would turn off cert validation on that
  production path too, making it MITM-able on any network between the job and the host — the one place you'd least want
  it. This gives you the same result for staging with one extra line.

  Notes:
  - [38;5;153mrequests[39m will emit [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m on staging runs. I left it in as a visible signal; say the word if you
    want it suppressed.
  - Files parse clean. [38;5;153mruff[39m reports two pre-existing [38;5;153mRUF100[39m unused-[38;5;153mnoqa[39m warnings in both scripts, unrelated to this
    change — left alone.
  - Not verified against a live endpoint (no network access to either host from here).

